   "text": "I traced the ring from the \u03b2-aminobutyric acid residue clockwise and wrote each residue as it appears in the drawing.\n\n**Ring sequence (clockwise from the top left):**\n1. \u03b2-Aminobutyric acid: NH\u2013CH(CH3)\u2013CH2\u2013C(=O)\n2. N-Me-Ala\n3. N-Me-Phe\n4. N-Me-Gly (sarcosine)\n5. N-Me-Leu\n6. Thr (NH, free OH)\n7. N-Me-Ala\n8. N-Me-Leu\n9. N-Me-Ile\n10. Ser(O-tBu) (NH)\n\nThe Ser carbonyl closes the ring onto the NH of the \u03b2-aminobutyric acid.\n\n**Stereochemistry:** I read each stereocentre from its wedge or hash and the positions of its neighbours in the drawing.\n- Every \u03b1-carbon (Ala, Phe, Leu, Ile, Thr, Ser) has a hashed H and comes out as the L-configuration, written `N[C@@H](R)C(=O)`.\n- The \u03b2-aminobutyric acid carbon, with a hashed H, comes out as `N[C@@H](C)C`.\n- The Thr \u03b2-carbon has a bold wedge to OH and comes out as `[C@@H](C)O`.\n- The Ile \u03b2-carbon has a hashed methyl and comes out as `[C@@H](C)CC`.\n\nI did not run this SMILES through a parser; I checked it by hand against the drawing.\n\nSMILES: N1[C@@H](C)CC(=O)N(C)[C@@H](C)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)C(=O)N(C)CC(=O)N(C)[C@@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H]([C@@H](C)O)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](C)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H]([C@@H](C)CC)C(=O)N[C@@H](COC(C)(C)C)C(=O)1"
